Z||000019||Taijiguy||03-30-2001||08:40 PM||||Well, this probably goes without saying, but be sure to check out the hydraulics thoroughly before yankin' everything to swap the clutch. If it is the clutch....well....what can be said about that kind of news....<BR>Good luck dude||||reg||1
Z||000020||Kelvin Vivian||03-30-2001||09:03 PM||||Have the clutch bled!<P>Problem: I could shift fine, but if I kept the clutch pressed to the floor too long, the motor would engage and the car would move... And then eventually it was tough to switch in between gears...<P>Solution: Had the clutch bled to remove air bubbles.<P>Result: No more shifting problems. And shifts like a new clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Philphine||03-30-2001||09:36 PM||||i wouldn't do it hibtually but you might be able to get home if there isn't a lot of shifting to be done to get there. my clutch went out coming home from work, but since i just needed to make it a short way to the express way i did the start the car in gear and turn it off (to start it in  gear again) at stops thing. i only had to stop for two lights though to get to the x way. then i jammed it through the gears and stayed in the steadiest moving lane i could (stay late and avoid rush hour if you have to). once i got off it's a couple of lights bettween the xway and home. don't know if that would work out for you but if it did at least you'd have the car at home.||||reg||1
